WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.528
In this video, you'll see where FedEx tracking appears — for you, for your staff, and for your customers.

00:00:06.978 --> 00:00:14.298
Every label comes with a FedEx tracking number. In the admin, they're listed in the Tracking ID column of Shipments and Printings.

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:22.380
Click Track in the Track Parcel column, and a popup opens — status, delivery date, service type, and the parcel's route.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:30.582
Every click asks FedEx for live data, so you're seeing the parcel right now, not a stored snapshot. The button becomes Track Again.

00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:38.280
The number is saved on the WooCommerce order too. There's no tracking panel on the order screen in this version — track from the list.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:46.122
Customers meet tracking in two ways: the shipment email, and whatever you put on a tracking page with the plugin's shortcodes.

00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:55.092
One shortcode renders a form where anyone enters a number. Another shows the scan-by-scan timeline for one order — stored scans only.

00:00:55.542 --> 00:01:04.734
Live lookups need their own credentials. In Settings, Tracking tab, enter a Tracking Client ID and Secret — empty fields fail quietly.

00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:12.072
On a sandbox store, the timeline can look empty — that's normal. Test labels never move real packages.

00:01:12.522 --> 00:01:18.474
That's tracking, from label to doorstep. Next: automate tracking updates with webhooks.

